module bindings.javascript.request_path_segment_screen;

// A wiki or CMS that stores its content under a root directory resolves a
// requested page or asset by decoding the request path, splitting it into
// segments, screening the segments, and re-joining them onto the storage
// root. Every spellings of the decode is documented: `querystring.unescape`
// decodes percent-encoding (and the legacy global `unescape` with it), which
// is the right treatment for a path that arrives encoded. The screening is
// what makes the function a screen rather than a splitter: dropping empty
// segments, trimming, rejecting the wrong locale — the author has declared
// the function the place where unsafe segments die.
//
// Dot segments are the one form such a screen must reject, because the
// re-joined path is resolved against a filesystem or storage root, and
// `path.join(root, '../../elsewhere')` resolves outside it. A screen that
// rejects empty segments or unknown characters while no dot-segment literal
// (`..`) appears anywhere in the function, and nothing normalizes or
// canonicalizes the path, passes traversal segments through untouched.
//
// The judgement lives in the pack rule (VYQL-PATH-041); this binding only
// recognises the shape of the screen. The conjuncts are node-level —
// literals and call paths come from AST nodes, so prose in a comment cannot
// satisfy the negated clause, and each route out is spelled by the code
// itself: rejecting `'..'`, or normalizing, both remove the first positive
// conjunct's weakness rather than rebranding it.
//
// Costs. The screen and the resolution live in different functions, so the
// binding cannot see where the re-joined path lands; a screen whose output
// never reaches a path resolution is reported with the same confidence as
// one whose does. Measured, the shape is rare: across the reachable set of
// every clone in the current corpus (any file carrying the decode call at
// all), the four conjuncts co-occur in one function only at the real defect
// — the decode-and-rejoin idiom in vendored client bundles splits its calls
// across functions. And the decode anchor is the `unescape` spelling only:
// widening it to `decodeURIComponent` was measured and rejected, as it
// reported two minified form-serialization bundles with no traversal in
// reach.

binding requestPathSegmentScreenMissingTraversal {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.context.language == "javascript"
    and node.context.callPath contains "unescape"
    and node.context.callPath contains "split"
    and node.context.callPath contains "filter"
    and node.context.callPath contains "join"
    and not (node.context.literal contains "..")
    and not (node.context.callPath contains "normalize")
  emit issue code.IncompleteDotSegmentRequestScreen at node
}
